"auto"},"review":{"blocking_severities":["critical","high"],"command":"env AP_REVIEWER_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5 AP_REVIEWER_EFFORT=low uv run python -B docs/examples/reviewers/claude_review.py","executor":"command","max_findings":50,"require_command_for":[]},"stage":{"max_attempts":5,"max_restarts":5,"timeout_seconds":1500},"worktree":{"copy_files":[".env"],"mode":"in_place","root":null,"setup_command":null}},"findings":[{"action":"auto_fix","code_owned":false,"detail":"derive_fix_commits drops empty commits in every mode. In in_place mode, respond sets doc.head_sha and doc.source_head_sha to new_commits[-1], which comes from _in_place_fix_commits and therefore from derive_fix_commits. Suppose the in-place checkout ends with an empty commit, such as a `git commit --allow-empty` marker after the fix. The recorded reviewed and source head is then the earlier non-empty commit, not the checkout's actual HEAD. Later freshness checks compare the recorded head with HEAD, and the next derivation spans the empty commit again. Before this change, commits_between kept every commit, so head_sha always advanced to HEAD. The skip exists only because cherry-pick replay stops on an empty commit, and in_place mode never replays commits.","fix_commit":"ead9f70dbbb5e97e724d08d0e27aad319c68a21b","id":"F001","line":77,"path":"agentic_preflight/runs/fix_commits.py","response_note":null,"severity":"medium","stage":"review","status":"fixed","suggestion":"Skip empty commits only when the mode is not in-place, as the merge check already does. Alternatively, have the in-place caller advance head_sha to the actual HEAD instead of new_commits[-1]. Add an in_place test in which the last commit is empty.","title":"Filtering empty commits also applies in in_place mode, so head_sha can be left behind HEAD","unit":"U0001"},{"action":"auto_fix","code_owned":false,"detail":"_register_validated_history runs before _require_state(run, State.TEST_GREEN, ...). Its docstring says it is correct only at TEST_GREEN, where review coverage names the checkout's current head. Merge-back can be invoked from another state, for example after the checkout gained commits that were never validated. In that case it persists a new fix_commits list and appends a validated_fix_commits_registered event that labels unvalidated commits as validated. Only then does it refuse the state.","fix_commit":null,"id":"F002","line":357,"path":"agentic_preflight/runs/mergeback.py","response_note":"_register_validated_history is called inside the `if run.state is State.TEST_GREEN:` block of mergeback (after evidence.advance and reopen_if_stale, which guarantee review coverage names the current validation HEAD), so it never runs from another state; MERGEBACK_PENDING/CONFLICT retries skip it.","severity":"low","stage":"review","status":"dismissed","suggestion":"Call _register_validated_history after the TEST_GREEN state check, or guard it with `run.state is State.TEST_GREEN`.","title":"Merge-back rewrites fix_commits before it checks that the run is at TEST_GREEN","unit":"U0004"},{"action":"auto_fix","code_owned":false,"detail":"In isolated modes, `_register_validated_history` runs before `_require_state(run, State.TEST_GREEN, ...)`. It calls `derive_fix_commits`, which can raise `StaleRun` or `NonlinearFixHistory`. It can also replace `fix_commits` in a transaction and add a `validated_fix_commits_registered` event. So `mergeback` called from the wrong state can change the run record, or return a history error instead of the expected wrong-state refusal. The docstring's reasoning only holds at `TEST_GREEN`.","fix_commit":null,"id":"F003","line":357,"path":"agentic_preflight/runs/mergeback.py","response_note":"Duplicate of F002 and not reachable: the call sits inside the `if run.state is State.TEST_GREEN:` block in mergeback, so a wrong-state mergeback reaches _require_state without deriving or writing fix_commits.","severity":"low","stage":"review","status":"dismissed","suggestion":"Call `_register_validated_history` only after the `TEST_GREEN` state check passes. You could also guard it with `run.state is State.TEST_GREEN`.","title":"Mergeback rewrites fix_commits before it checks that the run is at TEST_GREEN","unit":"U0004"},{"action":"ask_user","code_owned":false,"detail":"`_register_stage_fix_commits` now uses `record_fix_commits`, which returns only the SHAs it adds. In isolated modes, empty commits are filtered out. A repair made only of empty commits, or a rebase that only removes commits, returns an empty list even though the validation HEAD changed. Before this change, `commits_between` included every commit after the recorded stage head. Whatever branches on `commits` after this point may no longer return the run to review, while `entry.head_sha` is still cleared.","fix_commit":null,"id":"F004","line":137,"path":"agentic_preflight/runs/stages.py","response_note":"The restart does not depend on the returned list: _register_stage_fix_commits enters the restart branch on `record_entry.head_sha != current_head` and then unconditionally invalidates review and applies LINT/TEST_FIX_RESTART. `commits` is used only for the stage_fix_commits_registered event payload, so an empty-only repair or a rebase still returns the run to review.","severity":"low","stage":"review","status":"dismissed","suggestion":"Decide whether the restart decision should depend on a HEAD change (`current_head != record_entry.head_sha`) rather than on non-empty additions. Add a test for the empty-commit-only case.","title":"Whether the run returns to review now depends on newly added commits, not on whether HEAD moved","unit":"U0013"}],"fingerprint":{"base_tree_sha":"5b32ce4a99cfcb8e904870c04581cad61a8b47c1","config_sha256":"47de23995384e97c1f2cfdaf65f0f5392e876c6a60064aa0be584ac5e453621c","configuration_supported":true,"diff_sha256":"a5426b5731786a76fb4ec7bb70095c6390dc2df78bc788c336bb6bdfd8096a63","excluded_files":[],"executor":"command","grounding_sha256":"53f5dabf87c234377a8d33f69d94